Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four defects, each verified by rendering rather than by measurement.

1. Any page switch could black-screen the site permanently.
   _doShowPage calls heroEntrance, which called anime() unguarded — the one
   anime block on the site missing the typeof check every other one has. With
   the jsdelivr CDN slow, blocked, or its defer scripts not yet executed, the
   ReferenceError unwound out of _doShowPage and the two lines that retract
   the transition panel never ran. That panel is opaque #110F0E at
   z-index 9999, full viewport, and _pageTransitioning stayed true, killing
   every subsequent navigation. A second unguarded call site in the
   IntersectionObserver reveal threw continuously rather than once.

   Guards both call sites and wraps _doShowPage in try/finally so the panel
   retracts whether the switch succeeded or not. Skipping the animations is
   safe because every one of them animates opacity: [1,1] — no opacity change.
   A page without a transition is recoverable; a black screen is not.

2. The pathway wheel had been dead in production.
   show() wrote to .pw-hub-n/-t/-s, none of which exist in the markup — the
   Venn redesign dropped the centre hub from the HTML but not the JS. show(0)
   threw on every page load, so no stage highlighted and the entire panel
   never populated. Independent of the CDN. Writes the hub only if present.

3. Three Next Steps sections were invisible until scrolled to.
   .pw-detail started at opacity: 0 behind an IntersectionObserver — the fifth
   time this project has hidden content behind an animation. Entrance is now
   motion only, with a prefers-reduced-motion branch. The ui.js safety net
   stays, but nothing relies on it.

4. Events filter buttons failed WCAG AA.
   --text-light on the --u-200 events bar measured 4.34:1, the only place on
   the site that pairing occurs. --text-mid gives 5.33:1 and is the next stop
   on the existing ramp, so no new value enters the system.

Suite: 69 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed. Mobile was forced to Chromium because
WebKit is unavailable in this environment, so mobile is NOT verified on real
WebKit — see the note at the end of NEXT-SESSION.md.

The reported team-portrait bug does not reproduce; all twelve render. Details
and the evidence are in NEXT-SESSION.md.

